99d9-c6421e013ec3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138832 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186135940 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138832 #edb/ref 1069 "q-52749" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138832 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138832 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d7/d73138fe191ed536299fa6dca679e5948b389d26b4d9e32518eee79123f81f0e.png)\n\nGiven the diagram shown above, what is the measure of $\\overset{―}{BC}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138832 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9ea518d5-8976-5fea-b9cf-445893e46637" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186138832 #edb/ref 1074 "The segments addition postulate can be applied to a set of more than three collinear points. In the given situation, it states that\n\n$$\nAD=AB+BC+CD.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the known values into the equation above, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AD & =AB+BC+CD \\\\ 28 & =9+BC+7 \\\\ 28 & =16+BC \\\\ 28-16 & =BC \\\\ BC & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139556 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139556 #edb/ref 1061 8720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139556 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "11ec6bb7-b9b3-51ea-b1b2-5f6a0a7b75b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139556 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186139635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139557 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139557 #edb/ref 1061 8722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139557 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fa4d60ee-75e2-54e2-b7eb-e1946cf89388" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139557 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186139636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139558 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139558 #edb/ref 1061 8723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139558 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ab8e5676-8695-5fd6-8d78-6bae4eba68f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139558 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138558 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139559 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139559 #edb/ref 1061 8724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139559 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "df7668d0-a581-5a0f-a689-b2079711a0d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139559 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138559 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139560 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139560 #edb/ref 1061 8725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139560 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "24ee0c7c-ff8c-5b75-b742-a7f922b432ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139560 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139561 #edb/ref 1061 8726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139561 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e6dafd2a-98a4-508e-8294-b9f6ffd16d06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139561 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139635 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's look at the three points $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ below. \n\n![](images/95/956f9b2c979f06897b09119b089ced903e095d72136e64b9ea52e946fba41544.png)\n\nAs indicated in the diagram, the distances between the points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB=4\\,cm,\\,BC=2\\,cm,\\,AC=6\\,cm.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCan we be sure that the points are collinear? To answer this, we use the **segment addition postulate**.\n\nThe postulate states the following: \n\n*Three points are collinear if and only if the largest distance between two of them is equal to the sum of the other two distances.*\n\nHere, the largest distance is $AC,$ so the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ are collinear *if and only if*\n\n$$\nAC=AB + BC.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information $AC=6\\,\\mathrm{cm},$ $AB=4\\,\\mathrm{cm},$ and $BC=2\\,\\mathrm{cm}$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =AB+BC \\\\ 6 & =4+2 \\\\ 6 & =6.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a true statement, so the three points $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ are indeed collinear.\n\n**Note:** The postulate works in both directions:\n\n- If the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ are collinear then $AC= AB + BC.$\n\n- If $AC= AB + BC$ then the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ are collinear." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139635 #edb/ref 1065 2168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139635 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0a460529-da55-5fcc-a207-e4ae6a47f12d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139635 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139636 #edb/ref 1064 "Given two of the three distances between three collinear points, we can calculate the third distance using the segment addition postulate. \n\nTo demonstrate, consider the collinear points $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ below.\n\n![](images/3b/3b1bc383e8d570f9dc33346e6f4ae04377e3c5c4c0d0e53abe07b0aacd9bb3e6.png)\n\nWe are given two of the distances:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB=3\\,cm,\\,AC=5\\,cm\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the third distance, $BC,$ using the segment addition postulate.\n\nFirst, we state the equation and substitute the known quantities $AB=3$ and $AC=5,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =AB+BC \\\\ 5 & =3+BC\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for $BC$ by subtracting $3$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =3+BC \\\\ BC & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the points $B$ and $C$ is $2\\,\\textrm{cm}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139636 #edb/ref 1065 2169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139636 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "92286c39-6af9-5ff0-b2e9-fa44fec8f732" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139636 #edb/ref 1067 "Calculating the Distance Between Collinear Points" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557863 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186558582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557863 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186558652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557863 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186558658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557863 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557863 #edb/ref 1045 "The Segment Addition Postulate" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557863 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186558658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186557863 #edb/r